# Error handling

## Rules

| Context | On failure |
| --- | --- |
| Sourced [`bashrc`](../../bashrc) / predicates in [`lib/*`](../../lib/) | `return` non-zero (optionally print to stderr). **Never `exit`** — that kills the interactive shell. |
| Standalone scripts (`provision_init_files`, `install_node_toolchain`, `.github/ci/*`, …) | Fatal → [`init_files_die`](../../lib/error); warnings → `init_files_warn`; info → `init_files_log` (respect `init_files_log_quiet`). Top-level `exit` only for success/`--help` or via `die`. |

- User-facing failures go to **stderr**.
- Do not source `lib/error` from bashrc (avoids accidental `die` in interactive sessions).
- [`bootstrap_host`](../../bootstrap_host) stays curl/scp-able alone: keep an inline `die` with `SYNC: lib/error` (same idea as brew-bin SYNC in `lib/host_paths`).

## Anti-patterns

- `exit` inside bashrc functions (except content written into a heredoc for a **separate** executable wrapper).
- Duplicating `printf 'ERROR:'; exit 1` in scripts that already have `lib/error` available.
- Building a heavier “logging framework” than die/warn/log.
- Requiring every predicate `return 1` to also log.
